      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND CONTEXT",
          "content": "The association between scoliosis and spondylolisthesis is well documented, but criteria and methods for managing the deformity in patients with debilitating pain are not clear."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "To describe correction of scoliosis by internal fixation of the spondylolisthesis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "Case report of a patient with scoliosis developing in association with spondylolisthesis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A 17-year-old female presented with a 6-month history of back and leg pain. No spinal curvature was noted in radiographs taken at the onset of symptoms. Significant curvature appeared during the 6-month history of pain in association with an L5-S1 spondylolisthesis. The spondylolisthesis was repaired with posterior pedicle screw instrumentation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The pain and the spinal curvature almost completely resolved after surgery, and the outcome remained excellent 14 months after surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "In the properly diagnosed patient, surgical repair of the spondylolisthesis can relieve pain and correct spinal curvature."
        }
      ]},
